{"agent_id":"pp3g-bGeK-complete-the-hy3-review-of-the-workflow-topology-ba69","agent_seq":2,"timestamp":"2026-08-07T23:16:46.987348396Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"35c2be3c-b5f4-4d9a-9272-a3bb703d6600","comment_uuid":"138ef6bd-f641-4029-b0d3-5d337d0adc1f","author":"pp3g-bGeK-complete-the-hy3-review-of-the-workflow-topology-ba69","content":"Plan: Compose and post the hy3 (edge-case/source lens) verdict on the WORKFLOW-TOPOLOGY PROPOSAL in SMALL CHUNKS to defeat the 504 streaming failure that killed the verdict twice. Method: (1) Do NOT re-run the review — the analysis is preserved across #242 [VERIFY] (four load-bearing mechanical claims: reviewer.md bash allowlist has no python/no shell; hook-config agent_test_commands=[] so --verify ci is shellcheck-only; kickoff.md template carries no certainty obligation; playbook 6.2/6.3 already encode consumer-side output verification + producer self-review micro-gate) and #247 [PROGRESS] (four NEW mechanical findings: two-layer permission divergence on 'python '; shellcheck NOT INSTALLED so --verify ci is a no-op here; --kind vocabulary fixed with no certainty carrier; .opencode/permissions.md drifted from .opencode/agents/*.md both directions; plus source Rule #2 re-derived verbatim from syntheses/2026-06-23-beds24:39 and the 5c92e9d routing-table audit). (2) Cheap spot-confirmation only of the citations I quote verbatim, so the verdict's own certainty labels are honest — no new analysis axes. (3) Structure the verdict as numbered findings with severity across the four assessment axes (two principles / topology finding / STOPS A-B-C / model-agreement collection) and a PASS-CONDITIONAL-FAIL call. (4) POST AS 3 SEPARATE --kind result COMMENTS of ~1-2KB each ('VERDICT PART 1/3' principles+source-drift, 'PART 2/3' topology+STOPS, 'PART 3/3' model-agreement+conditions+verdict), syncing between chunks so a 504 on any one chunk loses at most that chunk and the rest survive. Review-only; no source files modified. NOT reading #243/#244/#245.","created_at":"2026-08-07T23:16:46.981365687Z","kind":"plan","signed_by":"SHA256:eY/brbXhEq7ai6H7oZOD+kB9yAWv+gXPpL4E1iMe0ZA","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgIcnsvrSqCctqGKfM7z9KDRMoMxSAXlfLD6+rFag/5H0AAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAW39gQKhrBCZv3YWGicSKVhIrMigFFDM+iCy+9cF3upDTKapAzPlGPEmqjpspMdSTukKiwSvHe+pGwiwaWH2uAw=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:eY/brbXhEq7ai6H7oZOD+kB9yAWv+gXPpL4E1iMe0ZA","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgIcnsvrSqCctqGKfM7z9KDRMoMxSAXlfLD6+rFag/5H0AAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQH8hUr/n2YeZEizK9sQx4mPUc7ROpFJAkcZKwKmdv/Oog5JD280FdqBjVyV4odAsMGWLFCgD9KKK3RhqtXFk1wI="}
{"agent_id":"pp3g-bGeK-complete-the-hy3-review-of-the-workflow-topology-ba69","agent_seq":3,"timestamp":"2026-08-07T23:16:59.958993981Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"35c2be3c-b5f4-4d9a-9272-a3bb703d6600","comment_uuid":"15dcd4f3-0f36-4fb0-a317-2b836920a5f3","author":"pp3g-bGeK-complete-the-hy3-review-of-the-workflow-topology-ba69","content":"[PROGRESS] state=working completed=plan posted; preserved findings from #242 [VERIFY] + #247 [PROGRESS] read and inventoried (4 original mechanical claims + 4 new ones + source Rule #2 verbatim + 5c92e9d routing audit) next=cheap spot-confirmation of verbatim citations, then compose and post VERDICT PART 1/3 blocker=none","created_at":"2026-08-07T23:16:59.952878056Z","kind":"observation","signed_by":"SHA256:eY/brbXhEq7ai6H7oZOD+kB9yAWv+gXPpL4E1iMe0ZA","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgIcnsvrSqCctqGKfM7z9KDRMoMxSAXlfLD6+rFag/5H0AAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAAeBI+aIuxTtmS7zzdlBJzq4nkFJgHtvW6LzkW2D65XUdK7Kb8cVpPkS3inD8nOOwB8017bGvBd2Pk/PVRGKfAw=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:eY/brbXhEq7ai6H7oZOD+kB9yAWv+gXPpL4E1iMe0ZA","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgIcnsvrSqCctqGKfM7z9KDRMoMxSAXlfLD6+rFag/5H0AAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQEvikX2bMyfQf2Knjr8xo3ycL8WzogHgSuIhP+hzAwlNxvND2jIpmyghkL9YlEsab+0nleJj/gtk6Lzg6mZRowA="}
{"agent_id":"pp3g-bGeK-complete-the-hy3-review-of-the-workflow-topology-ba69","agent_seq":4,"timestamp":"2026-08-07T23:19:36.109348849Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"35c2be3c-b5f4-4d9a-9272-a3bb703d6600","comment_uuid":"7a1a5748-5e97-41a6-8f42-0727d5f89191","author":"pp3g-bGeK-complete-the-hy3-review-of-the-workflow-topology-ba69","content":"VERDICT PART 1/4 (hy3, edge-case/source lens) -- WORKFLOW-TOPOLOGY PROPOSAL (#241).\n\nHEADLINE: CONDITIONAL PASS. The proposal is directionally correct and its two principles are genuinely load-bearing, but it is stated one abstraction level above the stack it must land on, and three of its mechanical preconditions are currently FALSE in this repo. 12 numbered findings across 4 axes; 8 blocking conditions in Part 4/4.\n\nBASIS: preserved analysis from #242 [VERIFY] and #247 [PROGRESS]. Every citation below was spot-confirmed live this session unless explicitly marked 'preserved'. Posted in chunks because this verdict was lost to a 504 streaming error twice.\n\nAXIS 1 -- THE TWO PRINCIPLES (source-fidelity lens).\n\nF1 [HIGH] PRINCIPLE (b) DRIFTS FROM ITS SOURCE, AND THE DRIFT IS ITSELF THE FAILURE MODE. Source re-derived verbatim at syntheses/2026-06-23-beds24-booking-system-evolution.md:39 -- 'Rule #2: Cheapest Falsifying Test First: Before writing complex code, execute the absolute cheapest test that can prove the core premise wrong.' The proposal restates this as the cheapest DISCRIMINATING test. Falsifying -> discriminating is a substantive weakening, not a paraphrase: a discriminating test merely separates two hypotheses, so an agent satisfies it with a cheap CONFIRMING check aimed at the premise it wants to be true. This is not academic pedantry -- the hub record contains the exact failure. In #226 -> #231 the marker-grep 'discriminated' (marker present vs absent) while wholly failing to falsify 'the build actually works'. The source rule would have caught it; the restated rule licenses it. Restore the source word.\n\nF2 [MEDIUM] PRINCIPLE (a) IS FAITHFUL BUT HAS NO CARRIER. It tracks its sources correctly (Core System Prompt v1/v2 reasoning standards; AGENTS.md evidence vs interpretation vs finding vs recommendation), and its 'state what was NOT tested' clause is a genuine ADDITION beyond source -- negative-space disclosure is the strongest single idea in the proposal. But the crosslink comment vocabulary is fixed and closed: note, plan, decision, observation, blocker, resolution, result, handoff, human (confirmed live). None encodes certainty. So principle (a) can only live in unstructured prose: unenforceable, unparseable, and -- critically -- not mechanically consumable by the model-agreement collection in Axis 4.","created_at":"2026-08-07T23:19:36.102405195Z","kind":"result","signed_by":"SHA256:eY/brbXhEq7ai6H7oZOD+kB9yAWv+gXPpL4E1iMe0ZA","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgIcnsvrSqCctqGKfM7z9KDRMoMxSAXlfLD6+rFag/5H0AAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABA/iKwNZcvId7EjAHwqfysP/r6mpRRgmf72wdnLxdsWKn8etyEp9ErsMB6jUlxw7h5Xeaq/c8fMtLGucIDSkc+Ag=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:eY/brbXhEq7ai6H7oZOD+kB9yAWv+gXPpL4E1iMe0ZA","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgIcnsvrSqCctqGKfM7z9KDRMoMxSAXlfLD6+rFag/5H0AAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQNwHoRGYtbh7PjmA6jccPV9a7T2qjDe497O0R6TerlyOASleeQQDssfjMLUAUdvtOdiSCOS6Pp0JhWUHnNgy8QI="}
{"agent_id":"pp3g-bGeK-complete-the-hy3-review-of-the-workflow-topology-ba69","agent_seq":5,"timestamp":"2026-08-07T23:20:06.764651773Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"35c2be3c-b5f4-4d9a-9272-a3bb703d6600","comment_uuid":"303f5e2d-1c43-4ff6-8ceb-6aba8bd1626b","author":"pp3g-bGeK-complete-the-hy3-review-of-the-workflow-topology-ba69","content":"VERDICT PART 2/4 (hy3) -- AXIS 2: THE TOPOLOGY FINDING.\n\nF3 [MEDIUM-HIGH] DIRECTIONALLY CORRECT, BUT 'DOMINANT' IS AN UNSUPPORTED QUANTIFIER offered with no denominator. The hub record supports 'a major, recurring class' -- #226 -> #231 false assurance, #182 vs #183 confident-and-wrong PASS, #235/#232 claim laundering, #240 brief-borne error. It does not support dominance as a measured claim. Worse, the same record contains three failure classes the topology does NOT cover:\n\n(a) NO-CLAIM FAILURES -- the silent hangs (#138/#142). Nothing crosses the boundary at all. A producer-side calibration duty cannot fire when there is no claim, and STOP B is by construction PRE-CONSUMPTION, so it never runs. The proposed corrective is null on precisely the failure mode that emits zero signal. This is the sharpest edge case in the proposal, and it is structural, not incidental.\n\n(b) BRIEF-BORNE ERRORS UPSTREAM OF THE PRODUCER (#240 arithmetic). The asymmetry INVERTS: the orchestrator authored the error and the producer inherits it as a given premise. Producer-side calibration cannot falsify what it was handed as an axiom. The proposal guards the producer->consumer edge but leaves the operator/orchestrator->producer edge unguarded.\n\n(c) MULTI-HOP LAUNDERING (#235/#232). STOP B guards ONE edge. A claim that clears hop 1 is re-emitted at hop 2 with the audit already marked 'done', and certainty is monotonically upgraded as provenance is progressively dropped. A single-edge gate cannot bound a multi-hop chain.\n\nAXIS 3 -- STOPS A/B/C, FEASIBILITY IN THIS STACK. This axis is where the CONDITIONAL originates. The stops presuppose verification capability the stack does not currently grant.\n\nF4 [HIGH] TWO-LAYER PERMISSION DIVERGENCE (confirmed live this session). .crosslink/hook-config.json top-level allowed_bash_prefixes DOES include 'python ', and the reviewer by_type override sets ONLY blocked_git_commands and gated_git_commands -- it does not restrict bash prefixes at all. So the crosslink hook layer PERMITS python to reviewers while .opencode/agents/reviewer.md (crosslink/opencode/git/ls/cat/cargo/npm/rtk, default deny) FORBIDS it. Effective capability is the undocumented INTERSECTION of two independently-drifting layers, and the drift runs both directions -- reviewer.md allows 'cat *' which the hook-config prefix list omits entirely, while .opencode/permissions.md has drifted from both. Consequence for the proposal: STOP B and STOP C cannot be costed, because no single file states the effective capability. Anyone costing STOP C from hook-config alone will over-estimate what a reviewer can actually run.","created_at":"2026-08-07T23:20:06.756952549Z","kind":"result","signed_by":"SHA256:eY/brbXhEq7ai6H7oZOD+kB9yAWv+gXPpL4E1iMe0ZA","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgIcnsvrSqCctqGKfM7z9KDRMoMxSAXlfLD6+rFag/5H0AAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAn451MlBBvGpBkKdkWIDisf4CXOunWTGvpQ4V+zjDLomflSgNviERNLqWWmU1x/uGaDmtX+nIcTcPDdekZ0DQAg=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:eY/brbXhEq7ai6H7oZOD+kB9yAWv+gXPpL4E1iMe0ZA","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgIcnsvrSqCctqGKfM7z9KDRMoMxSAXlfLD6+rFag/5H0AAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQD+Pod3TiRATvSPhWR3zMImzAhGowT5E43RlKeHWkW7G40qJb4Qfvi3aWS7lE3ho9W+KRRztVJxUx982pA7TGwg="}
{"agent_id":"pp3g-bGeK-complete-the-hy3-review-of-the-workflow-topology-ba69","agent_seq":6,"timestamp":"2026-08-07T23:20:39.848697440Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"35c2be3c-b5f4-4d9a-9272-a3bb703d6600","comment_uuid":"ae141327-7077-4449-8b81-60341fb1b985","author":"pp3g-bGeK-complete-the-hy3-review-of-the-workflow-topology-ba69","content":"VERDICT PART 3/4 (hy3) -- AXIS 3 CONTINUED.\n\nF5 [HIGH] STOP C's 'L2 RE-VERIFICATION' HAS NO SUBSTRATE IN THIS REPO. agent_test_commands is unset at BOTH the top level and in every by_type override, kickoff_verification is 'local', and shellcheck is NOT INSTALLED on this host (confirmed live). So 'crosslink kickoff run --verify ci' here is not merely thin -- it is a NO-OP. STOP C therefore degrades to another LLM reading the same text it just read. Labelling that 'L2' is a certainty inflation of exactly the kind principle (a) forbids: the proposal would violate its own first principle at its own highest-stakes gate (install/merge decisions). Either install the substrate or stop calling it L2.\n\nF6 [MEDIUM-HIGH] STOP B IS A CORRELATED CHECK, NOT AN INDEPENDENT ONE. Reviewers cannot execute here: this is a docs repo, so the cargo/npm entries in the allowlist are dead, and #199's tool_blocked intervention shows BOTH #196 reviewers falling back to fully static verification. A static second read by the same model population is correlated with the first, and correlated checks are precisely what #182 vs #183 demonstrates failing -- confident, agreeing, and wrong. STOP B as specified buys less independence than its position in the topology implies.\n\nF7 [MEDIUM] STOP B IS A SERIALIZING BOTTLENECK WITH AN UNBOUNDED LOOP. It sits on the single edge every result must cross, and the failure path (audit fails -> producer re-runs -> re-audit) has no iteration bound and no escalation rule. Under load this is the gate most likely to be skipped, which is the worst outcome: a gate believed to be holding while disabled.\n\nF8 [HIGH] UNTESTABLE CLAIM CLASSES ARE UNHANDLED -- the core edge-case gap. Absence and negative claims ('no other call sites', 'nothing else references this'), non-recurrence claims, latent and timing-dependent failures, judgement and design-quality claims, and cross-session durability claims admit NO discriminating test at any price. The proposal is silent on them, and silence is dangerous here: a standing cheapest-test DUTY pressures agents to manufacture a cheap proxy where no valid test exists. That is F1's marker-grep failure, now with policy backing. The proposal must explicitly require 'declare untestable + state residual risk' as a first-class, non-penalised outcome.\n\nF9 [MEDIUM] THE DELIVERY VEHICLE CARRIES NOTHING. The kickoff.md template has no certainty or cheapest-test obligation (preserved finding, verified across #242 and #247). It is the actual channel through which briefs reach agents; unamended, STOPS A and B are policy that never reaches the producer. Separately, the THIN-ORCHESTRATOR CONTRACT IS PRESERVED and this is a genuine PASS for the proposal: the orchestrator can read and judge under edit-deny, so a static STOP B is compatible with docs/ORCHESTRATOR.md -- but only a STATIC one. Any STOP B requiring execution breaks the contract, since the orchestrator bash map is crosslink / opencode models / read-only git / ls / cat / rtk.","created_at":"2026-08-07T23:20:39.842537154Z","kind":"result","signed_by":"SHA256:eY/brbXhEq7ai6H7oZOD+kB9yAWv+gXPpL4E1iMe0ZA","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgIcnsvrSqCctqGKfM7z9KDRMoMxSAXlfLD6+rFag/5H0AAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAUAeJD8x2ju86J42tSxpOhliW14fK9O6Mm6Scbw6Yu7gECtNCpyq0pDY/klChSXOA2driPQU2k39H2IxmHjTnBQ=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:eY/brbXhEq7ai6H7oZOD+kB9yAWv+gXPpL4E1iMe0ZA","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgIcnsvrSqCctqGKfM7z9KDRMoMxSAXlfLD6+rFag/5H0AAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQGHUxjIKoxzo4NV87wCtHPv6SNe859TJiQcNo12slMEA2ebnbhaDJ8uULEzCfWbHg1C+0+mCBjj9bfWOksAP2Q8="}
{"agent_id":"pp3g-bGeK-complete-the-hy3-review-of-the-workflow-topology-ba69","agent_seq":7,"timestamp":"2026-08-07T23:21:18.346630703Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"35c2be3c-b5f4-4d9a-9272-a3bb703d6600","comment_uuid":"4e82871f-5b0c-49af-85e7-7e97ca2c4964","author":"pp3g-bGeK-complete-the-hy3-review-of-the-workflow-topology-ba69","content":"VERDICT PART 4/4 (hy3) -- AXIS 4: MODEL-AGREEMENT COLLECTION, plus CONDITIONS and VERDICT.\n\nF10 [LOW-MEDIUM] 'ZERO-COST BYPRODUCT' IS TRUE AT VERDICT LEVEL ONLY. The orchestrator does already see all verdicts at synthesis, so recording verdict-level agreement genuinely is structuring existing work. But #241 itself states that FINDING-level agreement is the load-bearing part (the B2 divergence was masked at verdict level -- both CONDITIONAL, disagreed on the core issue). Finding-level agreement requires normalising each reviewer's findings into a shared claim-class taxonomy, i.e. the synthesiser making a semantic judgement that reviewer A's finding 3 IS reviewer B's finding 5. That is real, non-zero, judgement-heavy cost. Understating it is how collection gets silently skipped under load.\n\nF11 [MEDIUM-HIGH] SELF-REFERENCE / CONFORMITY LOOP -- the deepest design risk in Axis 4. Agreement data is collected BY the same orchestrator-plus-model population it measures, and is then fed into the Model-Routing-Matrix to route future work. Models that agree with the synthesiser score as reliable, get routed more, and agreement rises. The instrument then measures CONFORMITY, not correctness. Inter-model agreement is not a truth proxy: #182/#183 is an existing instance of confident agreement that was wrong. The schema needs an EXTERNAL CORRECTNESS ANCHOR -- did the finding later hold up -- recorded separately from agreement.\n\nF12 [MEDIUM] SURVIVORSHIP BIAS -- and this review is the demonstration. My verdict was lost to a 504 twice. Under the schema as drafted, a review whose verdict never lands produces no row, so the dataset silently over-represents reviews that completed and under-represents long, complex, high-finding-count ones. NON-DELIVERY (verdict lost / agent died / timed out) must be a first-class recorded outcome, distinct from 'no findings'. The 5c92e9d DRAFT artifacts are otherwise sound, and marking back-filled rows as lower-confidence reconstruction is correct and consistent with principle (a).\n\nVERDICT: CONDITIONAL PASS.\nThe two principles are right, the topology finding is real and useful, and STOP B is correctly placed. It fails to be an unconditional PASS because it is specified against an idealised stack.\n\nBLOCKING CONDITIONS:\nC1. Restore 'falsifying' (the source word) in principle (b). [F1]\nC2. Add the untestable-claim carve-out: declare untestable + state residual risk, non-penalised. [F8]\nC3. Drop the unqualified 'dominant'; explicitly scope out no-claim failures and brief-borne upstream errors; add a hop guard for multi-hop laundering. [F3]\nC4. Reconcile the three permission layers (hook-config / .opencode/agents/*.md / permissions.md) and cost STOP B and C against EFFECTIVE capability. [F4]\nC5. Stop calling STOP C 'L2' until an executable substrate exists here, or install one. [F5]\nC6. Add a certainty carrier -- kind extension or a mandated fixed prose header -- since the comment vocabulary has none. [F2]\nC7. Amend the kickoff.md template; bound the STOP B retry at n=1 then escalate. [F9, F7]\nC8. Record non-delivery as a distinct outcome and add an external correctness anchor. [F11, F12]\n\nNON-BLOCKING: state finding-level normalisation cost honestly rather than 'zero-cost' [F10]; declare STOP B static-and-correlated in this repo so its independence is not overclaimed [F6].\n\nNOT TESTED (per principle (a)): I did not measure STOP throughput cost empirically, did not validate the topology's 'dominant' claim against a full enumeration of hub failures (I sampled the known record), and did not read #243/#244/#245 by instruction, so this verdict i